1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,505
A detected face stays blurred until
someone rejects it,

2
00:00:03,505 --> 00:00:06,050
and approving it changes nothing on
screen.

3
00:00:07,350 --> 00:00:09,101
One rule decides what's hidden,

4
00:00:09,101 --> 00:00:11,135
and a single planner turns that into

5
00:00:11,135 --> 00:00:13,790
rectangles for both the preview and the
export.

6
00:00:19,350 --> 00:00:22,191
Every face starts as pending, and pending
is blurred,

7
00:00:22,191 --> 00:00:26,212
so approving just records that someone
checked, only a reject shows a face,

8
00:00:26,212 --> 00:00:29,590
and if you forget to review, every face it
found stays blurred.

9
00:00:34,350 --> 00:00:38,042
You can switch between pixelate, a black
box and blur live,

10
00:00:38,042 --> 00:00:40,670
and only the black box removes everything.

11
00:00:44,950 --> 00:00:48,539
Blurring a big face region directly took
sixty-two milliseconds,

12
00:00:48,539 --> 00:00:52,128
so instead I shrink it, blur the small
copy and enlarge it back,

13
00:00:52,128 --> 00:00:56,390
which takes one and a half milliseconds
and looks the same at this strength.

14
00:01:00,950 --> 00:01:04,630
Preview and export share every step that
decides what is hidden.

15
00:01:09,950 --> 00:01:12,990
Now, part two, getting it all into a file.

16
00:01:13,950 --> 00:01:19,110
If you export before a scan, the app stops
and explains, and No is the default.

17
00:01:27,550 --> 00:01:31,141
It counts which frames were scanned, names
the stretches that weren't,

18
00:01:31,141 --> 00:01:34,630
and rounds down, so it never shows a
hundred percent it didn't earn.

19
00:01:42,550 --> 00:01:47,034
Export works differently from playback,
because every single frame is decoded,

20
00:01:47,034 --> 00:01:51,230
in order, and nothing is ever dropped,
since the file has to be complete.

21
00:01:56,550 --> 00:01:58,745
Here is the 4K60 crowd exporting,

22
00:01:58,745 --> 00:02:02,670
and the progress counts frames FFmpeg has
actually written.

23
00:02:03,650 --> 00:02:06,903
My first export ran as one loop at fifty-
two frames a second,

24
00:02:06,903 --> 00:02:09,837
three stages on their own threads reached
seventy-four,

25
00:02:09,837 --> 00:02:13,410
and a faster H.264 encoder took it up to a
hundred and sixty-seven.

26
00:02:20,650 --> 00:02:25,350
That isn't FFmpeg beating OpenCV: OpenCV's
writer only offered OpenH264,

27
00:02:25,350 --> 00:02:29,396
so the app hands its frames to FFmpeg for
Microsoft's encoder,

28
00:02:29,396 --> 00:02:31,224
which also brings the audio,

29
00:02:31,224 --> 00:02:36,250
and a twenty-three-minute video now takes
about nine minutes, not twenty-six.

30
00:02:36,650 --> 00:02:41,595
The 4K60 crowd took nineteen seconds for
eight hundred and ninety-two frames,

31
00:02:41,595 --> 00:02:43,650
and the 1080p version just five.

32
00:02:49,750 --> 00:02:52,692
An unfinished export never gets the real
file name,

33
00:02:52,692 --> 00:02:57,191
because frames go to a temporary file
that's renamed only once the frame count

34
00:02:57,191 --> 00:03:00,710
matches, though a crash can leave that
temporary file behind.

35
00:03:05,750 --> 00:03:07,025
One bug here was mine,

36
00:03:07,025 --> 00:03:11,660
because my first FFmpeg export ran at
forty-nine frames a second while it waited

37
00:03:11,660 --> 00:03:15,194
for FFmpeg to read the whole twenty-three-
minute audio track,

38
00:03:15,194 --> 00:03:16,990
and padding the audio fixed it.

39
00:03:21,750 --> 00:03:24,684
At 4K, half of the decode is just
converting colours,

40
00:03:24,684 --> 00:03:27,231
and then the encoder converts them back
again,

41
00:03:27,231 --> 00:03:31,550
so keeping frames on the GPU would avoid
both, and that's where this is going.

42
00:03:38,750 --> 00:03:41,750
And when it's done, it tells you exactly
what it checked.

43
00:03:46,850 --> 00:03:50,287
Each check proves something specific and
says what it doesn't,

44
00:03:50,287 --> 00:03:54,610
so every frame is covered by the face
scan, but a model can still miss a face.

45
00:04:04,850 --> 00:04:09,190
So that's the whole export path, with
three tasks, two bounded queues,

46
00:04:09,190 --> 00:04:11,050
and FFmpeg doing the encoding.

47
00:04:13,850 --> 00:04:16,609
The costs are that a model can still miss
a face,

48
00:04:16,609 --> 00:04:18,580
so review and drawn regions are the

49
00:04:18,580 --> 00:04:21,170
backstop, and that the encoder uses CPU
cores.

50
00:04:31,850 --> 00:04:35,170
Next, the engineering behind all of it.

